<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Stimulus–response systems with simulation-preserving maps form a category" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "Stimulus–response dynamical systems (a state space, a stimulus space and an evolution law) together with pairs of smooth maps on states and stimuli that commute with time evolution form a category: identities satisfy the simulation condition and the condition is closed under composition, so a chain of locally valid scale transitions in a material hierarchy is globally valid." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "\\text{Obj: } (X, E, f),\\ \\dot x = f(x,e);\\ \\text{Mor: } (\\alpha, \\alpha_E) \\text{ with } d\\alpha_x\\big(f(x,e)\\big) = g\\big(\\alpha(x), \\alpha_E(e)\\big);\\ (\\beta,\\beta_F)\\circ(\\alpha,\\alpha_E) = (\\beta\\circ\\alpha, \\beta_F\\circ\\alpha_E) \\text{ again satisfies the condition}" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: finite-dimensional smooth state and stimulus manifolds, smooth evolution laws and maps. Formal status: proved (Marom, Tibbits, Zardini & Buehler 2026, Appendix A.2; the closure under composition is the chain rule applied to the simulation condition). This is a statement about the formal language, not about any material: the paper itself says that composition is verified by construction while the fidelity of each local model is empirical. The pinecone hierarchy fibre → lamina → tissue → element → organ is the worked instance, and the bilayer-curvature claim above is its element-scale law." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Not a claim about organisms; the empirical content lives in the local models it composes (hygromorph-bilayer-curvature, hygromorph-antisymmetric-twist). The paper's own validation is on printed bilayers (predicted vs measured opening angle within one standard deviation for four actuator classes), which is physical, not biological, data." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Lee Marom (2026) A category-theoretic framework from biological mechanics to engineered stimulus-response systems" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2009.0184> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/stimulus-response-systems-category> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.abi8919> .
}
